(function(){
  window.VN_LEARNING=window.VN_LEARNING||{};
  const M=(id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity)=>({id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity});
  const V=(title,kind,items)=>({title,kind,items}); const N=(title,cue,explanation)=>({title,cue,explanation}); const A=(prompt,options)=>({prompt,options}); const O=(text,correct,feedback)=>({text,correct,feedback});
  window.VN_LEARNING['lesson-5']={version:2,title:'Fats',chapter:'Dietary lipids + current evidence',duration:'95–115 minutes',
    outcomes:['Distinguish triglycerides, phospholipids and sterols and connect each with its role.','Explain why fat quality and food source matter more than a blanket low-fat rule.','Identify linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid as essential fatty acids.','Describe limited ALA conversion and the role of algae-derived EPA/DHA without mandating supplements.','Plan, store and cook with plant-fat foods while rejecting outdated ratio and seaweed claims.'],
    modules:[
      {id:'lipids',title:'Understand the lipid family',summary:'Connect structure with energy, membranes, signalling and transport.',moments:[
        M('vn5-m1','Lipids include several molecular families','Triglycerides store and deliver most dietary fat, phospholipids form membranes, and sterols include cholesterol and phytosterols.',[
          'Most fat in food and body stores is triglyceride: three fatty acids attached to glycerol. Phospholipids have a water-attracting region and fat-attracting region, which helps them form cell membranes and emulsions. Sterols have a ring structure; cholesterol is used in membranes and hormone synthesis, while plant sterols can reduce intestinal cholesterol absorption.',
          'Fat also supplies energy, helps absorb vitamins A, D, E and K, contributes essential fatty acids and affects flavour and satiety. These functions explain why “avoid fat” is not a sound vegetarian-nutrition goal.'
        ],['Triglycerides dominate dietary fat.','Phospholipids are membrane-forming amphipathic molecules.','Sterols include cholesterol and plant sterols.'],
        V('Lipid families','comparison',[{label:'Triglyceride',detail:'Three fatty acids + glycerol; energy storage.'},{label:'Phospholipid',detail:'Membranes and emulsification.'},{label:'Sterol',detail:'Ring structure; cholesterol or phytosterols.'}]),
        N('Three lipid shapes','TRI · PHOS · STEROL', 'Think stored fuel, membrane builder and ring-shaped regulator.'),
        'Dietary cholesterol occurs in animal-derived foods; vegan diets contain essentially none unless an unusual animal-derived ingredient is present.',
        'Explain the function of fat before discussing quantity so clients do not equate all fat with harm.',
        A('Which lipid is a major structural component of cell membranes?',[O('Phospholipid',true,'Its water- and fat-interacting regions form bilayers.'),O('Glycogen',false,'Glycogen is carbohydrate storage.'),O('Vitamin C',false,'Vitamin C is water soluble and not a lipid.')])
        ),
        M('vn5-m2','Fat source changes the nutrient package','The health effect of dietary fat depends on fatty-acid profile, food matrix and what the fat replaces.',[
          'Saturated, monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fats differ in chemical structure and effects. Replacing saturated fat with unsaturated fat generally supports cardiovascular health. Replacing it with refined carbohydrate does not provide the same benefit, so total-fat reduction can be misleading.',
          'Nuts, seeds, avocado and olives provide fat within a matrix that also contains fibre, minerals and phytochemicals. Oils are concentrated fats that can be useful for cooking and energy density but do not provide the same intact matrix. Coconut and palm fats are plant-derived yet relatively high in saturated fat.'
        ],['Replacement nutrient matters.','Whole plant-fat foods carry additional nutrients.','Plant origin does not guarantee low saturated fat.'],
        V('Fat-source matrix','spectrum',[{label:'Nuts/seeds',detail:'Unsaturated fats plus fibre, protein and minerals.'},{label:'Liquid oils',detail:'Concentrated fat with fatty-acid profile varying by oil.'},{label:'Coconut/palm',detail:'Plant fats relatively high in saturated fatty acids.'}]),
        N('Ask two fat questions','SOURCE + SWAP', 'Identify where the fat comes from and what it replaces.'),
        'Monounsaturated fats do not need to be avoided to reduce blood pressure; overall pattern and sodium are more relevant.',
        'Use substitutions the client can sustain—such as nuts instead of processed snacks or canola oil instead of butter.',
        A('Which swap generally improves fat quality?',[O('Butter to olive or canola oil.',true,'This usually replaces saturated fat with unsaturated fat.'),O('Walnuts to hard candy.',false,'This removes unsaturated fat and other nutrients.'),O('Olive oil to coconut oil solely because coconut is plant based.',false,'Coconut oil is higher in saturated fat.')])
        ),
        M('vn5-m9','Digest and transport fat through a water-based body','Bile disperses fat, pancreatic lipase releases fatty acids, micelles deliver them to intestinal cells and chylomicrons carry reassembled lipids through lymph and blood.',[
          'Large fat droplets do not mix readily with intestinal fluid. Bile salts emulsify them into smaller droplets, increasing the surface available to pancreatic lipase. Digestion products join micelles that approach the intestinal cell membrane. Inside the cell, many long-chain fatty acids are rebuilt into triglycerides and packaged with cholesterol, phospholipids and proteins into chylomicrons.',
          'Chylomicrons enter lymph before reaching the bloodstream, where lipoprotein lipase helps tissues take up fatty acids. Remnants return to the liver. This pathway also helps transport fat-soluble vitamins. Fat malabsorption, pancreatic or bile problems and some medications can therefore affect both energy and vitamins A, D, E and K—well beyond the simplistic idea that dietary fat merely becomes body fat.'
        ],['Bile emulsifies; lipase digests.','Micelles support delivery to enterocytes.','Chylomicrons transport dietary long-chain fat and fat-soluble compounds.'],
        V('Dietary-fat journey','flow',[{label:'Emulsify',detail:'Bile salts disperse fat droplets.'},{label:'Digest',detail:'Pancreatic lipase releases absorbable products.'},{label:'Absorb',detail:'Micelles deliver lipids to intestinal cells.'},{label:'Transport',detail:'Chylomicrons move through lymph and blood.'}]),
        N('Four stops for fat','EMULSIFY · DIGEST · ABSORB · TRANSPORT', 'Each step explains a different type of malabsorption problem.'),
        'Medium-chain fatty acids follow somewhat different transport routes; the chylomicron pathway describes most dietary long-chain fat.',
        'Persistent greasy stools, unexplained weight loss or fat-soluble-vitamin deficiency requires medical assessment—not simply adding more oil.',
        A('What is the main role of bile in fat digestion?',[O('It enzymatically cuts triglycerides into amino acids.',false,'Lipase digests triglycerides; amino acids come from protein.'),O('It emulsifies fat to increase the surface available to digestive enzymes.',true,'Dispersion supports efficient lipase action.'),O('It transports chylomicrons through blood.',false,'Chylomicrons are assembled in intestinal cells.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'essential',title:'Trace essential fatty acids',summary:'Distinguish omega-6 linoleic acid from omega-3 alpha-linolenic acid.',moments:[
        M('vn5-m3','LA and ALA are essential','Humans cannot insert double bonds at the omega-6 or omega-3 position, so linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid must come from food.',[
          'Linoleic acid, or LA, is the parent omega-6 fatty acid; alpha-linolenic acid, or ALA, is the parent omega-3 fatty acid. They contribute to membranes and serve as precursors for longer-chain compounds. Common vegetarian patterns usually provide abundant LA, while ALA may need more intentional food selection.',
          'Useful ALA sources include ground flax, chia, hemp, walnuts and canola or soybean oil. Leafy greens contain some ALA but usually not enough per serving to be the main strategy. Requirements concern adequate intake, not achieving one universal omega-6-to-omega-3 ratio.'
        ],['LA is the essential omega-6 fatty acid.','ALA is the essential omega-3 fatty acid.','Use concentrated ALA sources regularly.'],
        V('Essential-fat branches','pathway',[{label:'LA · omega-6',detail:'Common in many seeds and vegetable oils.'},{label:'ALA · omega-3',detail:'Flax, chia, hemp, walnuts and selected oils.'},{label:'Longer-chain products',detail:'Conversion occurs but is regulated and limited.'}]),
        N('Name the parents','LA = 6 · ALA = 3', 'Linoleic acid begins the omega-6 family; alpha-linolenic acid begins omega-3.'),
        'Avoiding every omega-6-rich oil is not current evidence-based guidance; the priority is ensuring adequate omega-3 and improving overall fat quality.',
        'Ask which ALA-rich food the person can use consistently rather than teaching a rigid ratio.',
        A('Which food is a concentrated ALA source?',[O('Ground flaxseed',true,'Flax is rich in ALA.'),O('Coconut oil',false,'Coconut oil is mainly saturated fat and low in ALA.'),O('Maple syrup',false,'It is not a meaningful fat source.')])
        ),
        M('vn5-m4','An “ideal ratio” is not the main goal','Absolute adequacy and food substitutions are more actionable than chasing a single omega-6-to-omega-3 number.',[
          'Older teaching often focused on lowering the omega-6-to-omega-3 ratio. Ratios can change because the numerator falls, the denominator rises or both, even when absolute intake is inadequate. Current guidance emphasizes meeting ALA needs, choosing unsaturated fats and limiting saturated fat rather than avoiding healthful omega-6 sources.',
          'High-dose supplement decisions, bleeding risk, medication interactions and pregnancy needs require individual assessment. The practical adult foundation is regular ALA-rich food, not fear of every seed oil.'
        ],['A ratio hides absolute intake.','Meet ALA needs first.','Do not convert a population discussion into a universal oil-avoidance list.'],
        V('Same ratio, different diet','comparison',[{label:'Low/low',detail:'Little LA and little ALA can produce a ratio that looks acceptable.'},{label:'Adequate/adequate',detail:'Both families may be sufficient at the same ratio.'},{label:'Action',detail:'Assess actual sources and amounts.'}]),
        N('Amounts before arithmetic','ALA FIRST, RATIO SECOND', 'A mathematically attractive ratio cannot rescue inadequate intake.'),
        'Sunflower oil is high in LA and low in ALA, but context and substitution matter; it is not automatically a forbidden food.',
        'Use food records and labels if quantitative assessment is needed; do not infer a ratio from one meal.',
        A('Why can a fatty-acid ratio mislead?',[O('It shows no information about absolute amounts.',true,'The same ratio can occur with very different intakes.'),O('LA and ALA are the same molecule.',false,'They belong to different essential-fat families.'),O('Ratios are always measured directly in the kitchen.',false,'They require composition and intake data.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'longchain',title:'Understand ALA conversion and direct sources',summary:'Use algae—not fish or ordinary sea vegetables—as the vegetarian direct source.',moments:[
        M('vn5-m5','ALA conversion to EPA and DHA is limited','ALA can be elongated and desaturated, but conversion—especially to DHA—is limited and variable.',[
          'EPA and DHA are long-chain omega-3 fatty acids. ALA can enter conversion pathways, yet much is oxidized or used in other processes, and conversion varies with genetics, sex, overall diet and metabolic context. This makes regular ALA intake important while preventing the assumption that a small serving guarantees a specific DHA level.',
          'DHA is highly concentrated in neural and retinal membranes, not in most cells of the body. The malformed source question should be corrected rather than forced into an “all statements” answer.'
        ],['ALA is the precursor.','Conversion to DHA is particularly limited.','DHA is concentrated in retina and nervous tissue.'],
        V('Omega-3 pathway','flow',[{label:'ALA',detail:'Essential plant omega-3.'},{label:'EPA',detail:'Limited conversion product with signalling roles.'},{label:'DHA',detail:'Further-limited conversion; important in neural and retinal membranes.'}]),
        N('Conversion narrows downstream','ALA → some EPA → less DHA', 'The pathway continues, but the proportion typically becomes smaller.'),
        'Statements about exact conversion percentages vary by study and should not be treated as fixed for every person.',
        'Use life stage, diet, health context and preferences when discussing direct DHA/EPA options.',
        A('Where is DHA especially concentrated?',[O('The retina and nervous tissue',true,'DHA is an important membrane fatty acid in these tissues.'),O('Every cell at ultrahigh concentration',false,'That wording is incorrect.'),O('Maple syrup',false,'Maple syrup is not a DHA source.')])
        ),
        M('vn5-m6','Microalgae provide direct vegetarian DHA and EPA','Algae-derived oils are the dependable vegetarian direct source; ordinary sea vegetables are not equivalent.',[
          'Fish accumulate EPA and DHA through aquatic food webs. Microalgae-derived oils can supply DHA and sometimes EPA directly without fish. Product dose and composition vary, so the label—not the word “algae”—determines what is provided.',
          'Nori, kelp and other sea vegetables are not generally reliable, substantial EPA/DHA sources and can introduce variable iodine. Presenting them as a direct omega-3 solution risks both inadequate long-chain omega-3 and excessive iodine.'
        ],['Microalgal oil is the direct vegetarian source.','Check whether the product contains DHA, EPA or both.','Sea vegetables are not reliable substitutes and may be high in iodine.'],
        V('Aquatic source chain','chain',[{label:'Microalgae',detail:'Produce long-chain omega-3s.'},{label:'Aquatic food web',detail:'Fish accumulate EPA/DHA.'},{label:'Vegetarian route',detail:'Purified algal oil supplies a direct source.'}]),
        N('Algae oil, not seaweed guessing','READ THE LABEL', 'Verify DHA/EPA amounts and serving rather than assuming every marine plant contains them.'),
        'Routine DHA/EPA supplementation is not mandated for every healthy vegetarian adult; individualize the decision.',
        'Pregnancy, infancy, medications and medical conditions require stage-specific or clinician-guided decisions.',
        A('Which is the most reliable direct vegetarian DHA source?',[O('A labelled microalgal DHA oil',true,'The product provides a measured direct dose.'),O('Any kelp salad',false,'DHA content is not reliable and iodine may be excessive.'),O('Leaf lettuce',false,'It is not a meaningful direct DHA source.')])
        ),
        M('vn5-m10','Make an omega-3 decision from life stage, diet and clinical context','Regular ALA-rich foods form the vegetarian foundation; a labelled algal DHA/EPA product is a direct option whose value depends on the person.',[
          'Start by documenting ALA sources and frequency rather than asking only whether someone takes “omega-3.” Check the supplement label for the actual amounts of DHA and EPA, serving size and other ingredients; “algae oil” alone does not specify the dose. Pregnancy, lactation, infancy, older age, very low fat intake, malabsorption or a diet with few ALA sources can change the discussion, but a direct supplement is not automatically mandatory for every healthy adult.',
          'Safety also depends on context. High-dose omega-3 products can affect bleeding and interact with antithrombotic therapy; products may add other nutrients, and more is not always better. NIH ODS notes that ALA conversion is very limited and that vegetarian supplements containing algal oil provide direct long-chain omega-3s. The responsible sequence is assess food, identify life-stage goal, read the product and coordinate medication or clinical questions.'
        ],['Assess ALA food intake first.','Read DHA and EPA amounts rather than relying on the front-label phrase “omega-3.”','Individualize for life stage, malabsorption, medications and clinical goals.'],
        V('Omega-3 decision path','decision',[{label:'Food base',detail:'Are concentrated ALA foods used consistently?'},{label:'Goal',detail:'General adequacy, pregnancy, treatment or another specific need?'},{label:'Direct source',detail:'Does a labelled algal product provide DHA, EPA or both?'},{label:'Safety',detail:'Dose, medications, bleeding risk and clinician guidance.'}]),
        N('Four omega questions','FOOD · GOAL · LABEL · SAFETY', 'This prevents both underplanning and automatic supplementation.'),
        'Cod-liver oil is not vegetarian and also contains vitamins A and D; it is not interchangeable with purified algal DHA/EPA.',
        'For pregnancy, infant feeding, anticoagulant use or diagnosed lipid disorders, connect general education with stage-specific professional guidance.',
        A('What should be checked before recommending an algal omega-3 product?',[O('Only whether the capsule is green.',false,'Colour does not establish content or safety.'),O('ALA intake, goal/life stage, labelled DHA/EPA amounts and medication context.',true,'These determine whether and how a product fits.'),O('Whether seaweed appears elsewhere in the diet.',false,'Sea vegetables do not reliably indicate DHA/EPA adequacy.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'foodskills',title:'Use fats safely in real meals',summary:'Balance adequacy, cooking function, storage and individual needs.',moments:[
        M('vn5-m7','Concentrated fats can be useful but are not mandatory','Adequate fat can come from whole foods, oils or both, depending on energy needs, appetite and cooking pattern.',[
          'Nuts, seeds, nut/seed butters, avocado, olives, soy foods and oils can all contribute fat. Oils are not required for every person, yet they can increase energy density, improve palatability and support absorption of fat-soluble carotenoids—especially when appetite or food volume is limited.',
          'Infants, children, athletes and people restoring weight may need intentional energy density. Someone managing energy intake may emphasize whole-food fat sources and measured oils. A low-fat rule should not override essential-fat and energy needs.'
        ],['Whole foods and oils can both contribute.','Needs differ by appetite, life stage and energy goal.','Some dietary fat supports absorption of fat-soluble compounds.'],
        V('Fat-planning tools','toolkit',[{label:'Whole-food fats',detail:'Nuts, seeds, avocado, olives and soy foods.'},{label:'Concentrated fats',detail:'Oils and spreads for energy and culinary function.'},{label:'Individual fit',detail:'Volume tolerance, goal, culture and budget.'}]),
        N('Use the right tool','WHOLE · POUR · PURPOSE', 'Choose a whole-food fat or oil for a clear nutritional or culinary purpose.'),
        'Nuts are associated with favourable cardiometabolic outcomes in many studies; they are not shown to increase diabetes and heart-disease risk as the source question suggests.',
        'For allergy, swallowing difficulty or pediatric use, choose an age-appropriate form and avoid choking hazards.',
        A('Who may particularly benefit from intentional energy-dense fats?',[O('A child with limited stomach capacity and adequate allergy-safe options.',true,'Energy-dense foods can help meet needs in a small volume.'),O('Everyone must drink oil.',false,'Needs and preferred sources vary.'),O('No vegetarian at any life stage.',false,'Fat is essential and energy density can be important.')])
        ),
        M('vn5-m8','Unsaturated fats need thoughtful storage and heat use','Light, oxygen, heat and time can oxidize fats, especially highly polyunsaturated oils, nuts and seeds.',[
          'Rancidity produces unpleasant flavours and signals lipid oxidation. Store nuts, seeds and vulnerable oils sealed away from heat and light; refrigeration or freezing can extend storage for some products. Buy quantities that match use rate and follow manufacturer guidance.',
          'Cooking suitability depends on oil composition, refinement and temperature. Avoid repeatedly overheating oil or using it after obvious deterioration. “Smoke point” is useful but not the only stability measure, and cold-pressed is not automatically superior for every cooking task.'
        ],['Oxygen, light, heat and time promote oxidation.','Storage should match fat vulnerability and use rate.','Repeated overheating is undesirable.'],
        V('Oxidation controls','controls',[{label:'Seal',detail:'Limit oxygen exposure.'},{label:'Shade',detail:'Protect from light and heat.'},{label:'Cool',detail:'Refrigerate/freezer-store vulnerable products when suitable.'},{label:'Cycle',detail:'Use fresh stock before buying more.'}]),
        N('Four S storage check','SEAL · SHADE · STORE COOL · SMALL BATCH', 'Reduce oxidation by limiting exposure and time.'),
        'A stale or paint-like odour is a practical reason to discard a rancid nut or oil product.',
        'Teach storage as food-quality and safety practice, not as a reason to fear unsaturated fats.',
        A('What is the best storage approach for ground flax?',[O('Open beside a hot stove for months.',false,'Heat, light and oxygen accelerate deterioration.'),O('Sealed and cool, following product guidance and using it regularly.',true,'This reduces exposure and storage time.'),O('Mixed with water and left at room temperature indefinitely.',false,'That is not safe or stable.')])
        )
      ]}
    ],
    sources:[
      {title:'NIH Office of Dietary Supplements — Omega-3 Fatty Acids',url:'https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Omega3FattyAcids-HealthProfessional/'},
      {title:'American Heart Association — Fats in Foods',url:'https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/healthy-eating/eat-smart/fats/fats-in-foods'},
      {title:'Health Canada — Canada’s Dietary Guidelines',url:'https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-guide/explore/dietary-guidelines/section-1-foundation-healthy-eating.html'},
      {title:'Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics — Vegetarian Dietary Patterns for Adults (2025)',url:'https://www.eatrightpro.org/-/media/files/eatrightpro/practice/academy-positions/vegetarian-dietary-patterns-for-adults.pdf'}
    ],
    caseStudy:{title:'The omega-ratio overhaul',scenario:'Leo avoids soybean, canola and sunflower oil because an old handout says all omega-6 is inflammatory. He uses coconut oil for every meal, eats no ALA-rich foods and assumes kelp supplies DHA.',prompts:['Which three beliefs need correction?','What food-based ALA plan would be practical?','When might a labelled algal DHA/EPA product be discussed?'],takeaway:'Meet essential-fat needs with regular ALA-rich foods, improve overall fat quality and use measured algal products—not ratio fear or kelp—as the direct vegetarian DHA/EPA option.'},
    recap:['Lipids include triglycerides, phospholipids and sterols.','Fat digestion follows emulsify, digest, absorb and transport.','Fat quality and substitution matter more than a blanket low-fat rule.','LA and ALA are essential.','Absolute intake matters more than one ratio.','ALA conversion to DHA is limited.','Labelled microalgal oil—not ordinary sea vegetables—is the direct vegetarian DHA/EPA source.','Omega-3 decisions should consider food intake, goal, product label and safety.','Storage protects unsaturated fats from oxidation.'],
    flashcards:[{front:'Triglyceride',back:'Glycerol plus three fatty acids; the main form of dietary and stored fat.',tag:'lipids'},{front:'Phospholipid',back:'Amphipathic lipid central to cell membranes and emulsification.',tag:'lipids'},{front:'Sterols',back:'Ring-structured lipids including cholesterol and phytosterols.',tag:'lipids'},{front:'Fat digestion sequence',back:'Emulsify, digest, absorb and transport.',tag:'digestion'},{front:'Bile',back:'Emulsifies dietary fat, increasing the surface available to lipase.',tag:'digestion'},{front:'Chylomicron',back:'A lipoprotein assembled in intestinal cells to transport dietary long-chain lipids through lymph and blood.',tag:'transport'},{front:'Essential omega-6',back:'Linoleic acid (LA).',tag:'essential'},{front:'Essential omega-3',back:'Alpha-linolenic acid (ALA).',tag:'essential'},{front:'Concentrated ALA sources',back:'Ground flax, chia, hemp, walnuts and canola or soybean oil.',tag:'ALA'},{front:'ALA conversion',back:'Conversion to EPA is limited and conversion to DHA is generally more limited.',tag:'conversion'},{front:'DHA-rich tissues',back:'Retina and nervous tissue.',tag:'DHA'},{front:'Direct vegetarian DHA/EPA',back:'Labelled microalgal oil.',tag:'DHA'},{front:'Sea vegetables',back:'Not reliable EPA/DHA sources; iodine can be highly variable.',tag:'correction'},{front:'Omega-3 decision check',back:'Food, goal, label and safety.',tag:'planning'},{front:'Ratio limitation',back:'It hides absolute amounts and can look favourable even when both fats are low.',tag:'evidence'},{front:'Rancidity controls',back:'Limit oxygen, light, heat and storage time.',tag:'storage'}],
    practice:[{tag:'lipids',prompt:'Name the three major lipid families taught here.',answer:'Triglycerides, phospholipids and sterols.',rationale:'They have distinct structures and roles.'},{tag:'digestion',prompt:'Trace dietary long-chain fat in four verbs.',answer:'Emulsify with bile, digest with lipase, absorb via micelles/enterocytes, and transport in chylomicrons.',rationale:'The sequence connects digestion with circulation.'},{tag:'replacement',prompt:'Why is “total fat” an incomplete health measure?',answer:'Fatty-acid type, food matrix and what the fat replaces influence outcomes.',rationale:'Equal grams can have different contexts.'},{tag:'essential',prompt:'Name the two essential fatty acids.',answer:'Linoleic acid (LA) and alpha-linolenic acid (ALA).',rationale:'Humans cannot synthesize their omega-6/omega-3 double-bond positions.'},{tag:'ALA',prompt:'Build one daily ALA habit.',answer:'Examples include adding ground flax or chia, eating walnuts, or using canola oil regularly.',rationale:'Consistency supports adequacy.'},{tag:'ratio',prompt:'Why can an omega ratio look good while intake is poor?',answer:'Both numerator and denominator can be very low.',rationale:'Absolute intake is hidden.'},{tag:'conversion',prompt:'Trace the omega-3 conversion path.',answer:'ALA can convert to some EPA and then smaller amounts of DHA.',rationale:'Downstream conversion is limited and variable.'},{tag:'DHA',prompt:'Correct “sea vegetables are a great EPA/DHA source.”',answer:'Ordinary sea vegetables are not reliable direct sources; labelled microalgal oil provides measured vegetarian DHA/EPA.',rationale:'Source and dose must be verifiable.'},{tag:'planning',prompt:'Apply the four-question omega-3 decision check.',answer:'Assess ALA-rich foods, clarify the goal/life stage, read labelled DHA/EPA amounts, and review medications/safety.',rationale:'This avoids automatic or underspecified supplementation.'},{tag:'planning',prompt:'When might oil be useful?',answer:'For cooking, palatability, carotenoid absorption or extra energy density when food volume is limited.',rationale:'Concentrated fat has practical roles.'},{tag:'storage',prompt:'List four factors that accelerate fat oxidation.',answer:'Oxygen, light, heat and time.',rationale:'Storage can control all four.'},{tag:'synthesis',prompt:'Summarize current vegetarian omega-3 guidance.',answer:'Use ALA-rich foods regularly and individualize labelled algal DHA/EPA rather than chasing a universal ratio.',rationale:'This is current, practical and evidence calibrated.'}]
  };
})();
